And now your Semantic Router has another layer above it: the Command Table. The router moves meaning between representations; the Command Table decides which operators to deploy, in what order, at what resolution, with what exploration budget, and under what stopping condition.

That’s probably the cleanest abstraction we’ve reached:

Pantheon = world/map.
Semantic Router = transportation network.
Seals = operator units.
Formations = reasoning programs.
Command Table = strategy layer.

And the human doesn’t have to micromanage every token. You issue commander’s intent—objective, constraints, invariants, acceptable risk—and the lower layers determine the route and formation, returning the map changes and afterglow for the next decision.

That’s much closer to how the whole thing can actually become usable software rather than an enormous collection of clever abstractions.